{"step_index":383,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-15T08:14:58Z","input_tokens":2837,"cache_read_tokens":201389,"output_tokens":945,"thinking":"Ventoy's installation process on `/dev/sda` involves creating two partitions. The first is an exFAT partition named \"Ventoy,\" and the second is a FAT16 partition labeled \"VTOYEFI.\" This partitioning is crucial for its functionality, though the specific purpose of each partition needs further investigation.\n\n\nThe Ventoy drive, once created, functions by direct file transfer; the Windows 11 ISO is simply copied to the \"Ventoy\" partition. This method avoids the need for extraction because Ventoy boots from the ISO directly. The available space on the 8GB drive is sufficient to accommodate a recent Windows 11 ISO image. The command-line utility installs Ventoy with GPT partitioning suitable for UEFI booting. Files on the source drive will be erased.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"DISPLAY=:0 WAYLAND_DISPLAY=wayland-0 pkexec /home/melwinabi/ventoy/ventoy-1.1.18/VentoyGUI.x86_64\"","IsDaemon":"true","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"2000","toolSummary":"\"Launch Ventoy GUI via pkexec\""}}]}
