{"step_index":403,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-15T08:18:20Z","input_tokens":2225,"cache_read_tokens":207132,"output_tokens":1082,"content":"**Rufus only exists for Windows (`.exe`)**—there is no version of Rufus that runs on Ubuntu/Linux *(even running it through Wine fails because Wine cannot access raw USB hardware)*.\n\nOn Linux, the two official tools used to do the exact same job as Rufus are:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Linux Equivalents to Rufus\n\n| Tool | How it works |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Ventoy** *(Already downloaded on your PC)* | **The modern standard:** Formats `/dev/sda` with UEFI/GPT, supports Secure Boot, and bypasses Windows 11 TPM/RAM requirements automatically. You just copy the Windows 11 ISO file into the USB. |\n| **WoeUSB** | The classic \"Rufus for Linux\" command-line tool that formats the drive and extracts the Windows ISO. |\n\n---\n\n### 2. To Proceed, You Need the Windows 11 ISO File\n\nWhether using Ventoy, WoeUSB, or Rufus, none of them can make a bootable drive without the **Windows 11 ISO file** (~5.5 to 6.2 GB).\n\nBecause Microsoft blocks automated terminal downloads with bot-protection, you need to trigger the download in your browser:\n\n1. Look at the **Google Chrome** window open on your screen (or open [**microsoft.com/software-download/windows11**](https://www.microsoft.com/software-download/windows11)).\n2. Scroll to: **Download Windows 11 Disk Image (ISO) for x64 devices**.\n3. Select **Windows 11 (multi-edition ISO for x64 devices)** ➔ Click **Download Now**.\n4. Choose language: **English (United States)** ➔ Click **Confirm**.\n5. Click **64-bit Download**.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Format the Pendrive (Takes 5 Seconds)\n\nWhile the ISO is downloading in Chrome, open your terminal (`Ctrl + Alt + T`) and run this command to format your Cruzer Blade USB with GPT/UEFI bootloader:\n\n```bash\nsudo /home/melwinabi/ventoy/ventoy-1.1.18/Ventoy2Disk.sh -I -g /dev/sda\n```\n\nOnce Chrome finishes downloading the Windows 11 ISO, simply drag and drop the `.iso` file into your newly formatted **`Ventoy`** USB drive in your file manager. \n\nYour bootable Windows 11 pendrive will be 100% complete and ready to install!","thinking":"Rufus is a Windows-specific application; there is no native Linux build. Attempts to use Rufus with a compatibility layer fail due to limitations in direct hardware access under that environment. The equivalent, and readily available, solution is Ventoy, which provides similar functionality, including GPT/MBR partition creation for bootable USB drives.\n\n\n"}
